{"kind":"report","text":"Completed preliminary static review of the one cloud-disposition note at exact 7aa072a7d0be0fdfe65c530b93571caf29d23c38, notes/2026-10-05-review-dispositions.md. This completes checker promise 9217a5c7 under b3cc4f92. The planner has already accepted delivery 4bd9d4a1 through 71d695c2 and commissioned the current-product F1–F5 lane under c5cc42bd; these corrections preserve those lanes and do not reopen acceptance or authorize source changes here.\n\nCoverage is exhaustive: all 64 unique expected rows are present with no missing/duplicate/unexpected IDs: M1–7, L1–14, all ten Info, S1–5, D1–12, K1–7 and N1–9. Claimed route totals match: 43 Design, 16 Design owed, 2 Fix and 3 Accepted. Root and independent guidance auditor read the complete 197-LF/38,424-byte note (SHA-256 a1b85aacb9481e1004a4cf625cca2435a532cdf8695b3b5be75d16d7c3481fa8) and compared the already fully read security/layering bodies at 22b593c7. Four P2 route/correction findings remain.\n\n1. F3 line 179 needs distinct mechanisms and evidence for Git and MCP. The cited CLI example is actually claude mcp add --header (22b593c7 packages/cli/src/main.ts 745–750), not a Git invocation. Git configuration variables can replace the workspace Git token example but cannot by themselves configure that MCP authorization header. Specify a credential-safe mechanism appropriate to the actual MCP client/configuration, or remove that unsafe example with accurate usable guidance, without substituting an unverified command. The proposed proving test only reads MCP tool descriptions; it does not verify the CLI output that F3 also changes. Cover that actual second output within the existing compact F3 evidence, rather than adding a separate heavy suite or retaining a false example. Keep no token in process arguments. This corrects the already commissioned c5cc42bd F3 specification, not a new fix batch.\n\n2. M6 line 65 says its witness already exists at R4 O4, but that acceptance is device configuration/shell/property safety (c3e699a6 invariants.md 157; main 1096), not attribution of tool-result/summary prose or inability of that prose to grant control authority. The R3 data boundary is correctly marked owed. Its actual boundary proof must also be marked owed and routed into the existing R4/I5/IA follow-through, reusing or extending an appropriate compact boundary row with explicit assertions rather than claiming the present O4 table demonstrates them. F1 line 177 is an instruction-presence mitigation; a sentence within 512 characters cannot certify the complete M6 data/authority boundary.\n\n3. Info 6 line 91 addresses session expiry/restart but omits abandoned-reader cancellation cleanup. Adopted R4 already owns the exact acceptance at main 1427 and invariants.md 170: pending read completes, the owned subscription releases once, and no reconnect follows disposal. Route to that existing O12 table and its client/service owners; do not replace immediate cleanup by waiting for session expiry. Preserve R3/R4's explicit clock and failed-check qualification for session/revocation stopping (main 1428); ten minutes of session policy alone is not a universal real-time bound.\n\n4. L13 line 84 lists all four verifier subissues but assigns them all to canonical parsing/coverage and deletion of verify.ts/decode.ts in I1 S5. The original security review 602–612 separately identifies Git object-type/parent-format checking and safe object-ID arguments. The I1 plan at 3ed99af5 line 114 retains log/git.ts and gitcli.ts for I3, conditional on I1 replay needing them. Current parked/log/src/git.ts 129–144 still accepts parent text by cast; parked/log/src/gitcli.ts 87–90 passes object identifiers to cat-file. Those boundaries are not discharged by canonical JSON parsing or deleting the verifier/decoder. Give the four subissues separately sufficient routes/owners: coverage/consistent-prefix reporting, malformed object/parent handling, validated Git argument IDs and canonical line form. Reuse R4 O15 main 1202–1203/invariants 173 for malformed objects and pre-Git validation; assign the retained adapter review to I3 and I1 only when it actually reuses them. No claim of demonstrated command execution or provider effects.\n\nOther route qualifications to retain: M1's owed rule must preserve the adopted A-revoked/B-active versus last-key/member-removal distinctions (R4 main 1416–1417 and existing da310403), not end all hosted work on every device revocation. M3/M4 must preserve valid invitations under hostile load and keep transient limiter eviction nonauthoritative. L7 correctly preserves unknown provider creates and returned-identity duties. Layering K6 correctly records the intentional shared pure judge with independence in inputs/identity/anchors/trust; no duplicate decision engine is warranted. Source parking does not establish retirement of deployed/packed products, and an accepted exposure is not a source fix. R1 revision 8 cited by this note remains a candidate; R3 revision 10 remains owed; current R1 revision 9 requires its own review. None of the listed design routes proves implementation or outcomes.\n\nRead limits: every disposition byte and original finding inventory/body were read; relevant old-source and adopted-design citations were selectively inspected, not a fresh full audit of every cited package or third-party implementation. Root independently reread CLI 736–757; R4 main 1090–1115,1193–1210,1410–1435 and invariants150–174; I1 plan106–119; parked Git129–144/gitcli71–99; compound L13/Info and layering carry/do-not-repeat sections. No project/tests/build/install/private diagnostic execution, source edit, merge, deploy, message to external people or provider effect. Original cloud test/command claims remain attributed evidence. This is a document/evidence correction report, not ordinary source approval or a new gate; all complete source/runtime/removal/application/cloud/browser/device/operator/unknown-effect duties remain owned.